            <ArticleTitle>Does dance-based therapy increase gait speed in older adults with chronic lower extremity pain: a feasibility study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>339-44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.gerinurse.2014.03.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0197-4572(14)00142-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A decreased gait speed in older adults can lead to dependency when the individuals are no longer able to participate in activities or do things for themselves. Thirty-seven senior apartment residents (31 females; Mean age=80.6 years; SD=8.9) with lower extremity pain/stiffness participated in a feasibility and preliminary efficacy study of 12 weeks (24 sessions). Healthy-Steps dance therapy compared to a wait-list control group. Small improvements in gait speed ([ES]=0.33) were noted for participants completing 19-24 dance sessions. Improvements in gait speed measured by a 10 Meter Walk Test (0.0517 m/s) exceeded 0.05 m/s, a value deemed to be meaningful in community dwelling older adults. These feasibility study findings support the need for additional research using dance-based therapy for older adults with lower extremity pain.</AbstractText>
